{
 "slug": "simplifying-expressions",
 "title": "Simplifying Expressions: Like Terms, Expanding Brackets and Factorising",
 "seo_title": "Year 7 Simplifying Expressions Worksheets: Collecting Like Terms, Expanding Brackets and Factorising",
 "short_title": "Simplifying expressions",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "KS3: Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions to maintain equivalence by: collecting like terms; multiplying a single term over a bracket; taking out common factors.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 7 worksheets on simplifying expressions: collecting like terms, multiplying terms, expanding single brackets, expanding and simplifying, and factorising. 100 questions with step-by-step answers.",
 "key_big": "Only like terms can be collected: 3x + 5x = 8x, but 3x + 2y stays as it is.",
 "key_sub": "Expand by multiplying every term in the bracket. Factorise by taking out the highest common factor.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p><b>Like terms</b> have exactly the same letters and powers. Only like terms can be added or subtracted: 3<i>x</i> + 5<i>x</i> = 8<i>x</i>, but 3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> cannot be simplified. The sign belongs to the term after it.</p><p>To <b>expand</b> a bracket, multiply every term inside by the term outside. To <b>factorise</b>, take out the highest common factor and put the rest in a bracket.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify 5<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> − 2<i>x</i> + <i>y</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i> terms: 5<i>x</i> − 2<i>x</i> = 3<i>x</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>y</i> terms: 3<i>y</i> + <i>y</i> = 4<i>y</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand and simplify 3(<i>x</i> + 2) − 2(<i>x</i> − 4).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>x</i> + 6 − 2<i>x</i> + 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Collect: <i>x</i> + 14.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> + 14</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factorise 12<i>a</i> + 18.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>The HCF of 12 and 18 is 6.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>12<i>a</i> ÷ 6 = 2<i>a</i> and 18 ÷ 6 = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 6(2<i>a</i> + 3)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Combining unlike terms: 3<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> is not 5<i>xy</i>.</li><li>Multiplying only the first term: 4(<i>x</i> + 3) is 4<i>x</i> + 12, not 4<i>x</i> + 3.</li><li>Losing the sign: −2(<i>x</i> − 4) = −2<i>x</i> + 8.</li><li>Writing <i>x</i> × <i>x</i> as 2<i>x</i>. It is <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What are like terms?",
   "a": "Terms with exactly the same letters and powers, such as 3x and −5x. 3x and 3x² are not like terms."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can I simplify 3x + 2y?",
   "a": "No. They are unlike terms, so 3x + 2y is already as simple as it can be."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I expand a bracket with a negative in front?",
   "a": "Multiply each term by the negative number. −2(x − 4) = −2x + 8, because −2 × −4 = +8."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does factorise fully mean?",
   "a": "Take out the highest common factor, so nothing more can come out of the bracket. 12a + 18 = 6(2a + 3), not 2(6a + 9)."
  }
 ],
